From fd3cdddce99cbc4ca30e01e0c72e7de5c1e1a39d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 15:39:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NSPredicateEditor: one set of pop-ups over all its templates,
 as on OS X

A predicate editor made each row from the views of one template, so a row
offered only that template's left expressions and operators, and could
never become a row of another template.  NSPredicateEditorRowTemplate's
+templatesWithAttributeKeyPaths:inEntityDescription: returned nil, which
left an editor for a Core Data entity with nothing to offer.  The rows had
a plus button and no minus, and nothing a user did sent the editor's
action.  The editor, the rule editor and the templates also lost
everything a xib or a keyed archive said about them, and a xib connecting
an editor's action could not be loaded at all: NSRuleEditor declared
_target and _action but had no accessors, so -setTarget: went to
NSControl's, which hand it to the cell, and a plain NSCell raises.

What OS X 15 does with the same templates and steps:

- A rule editor keeps the target and the action it is given, and giving a
  predicate editor a predicate does not send it.
- A rule editor walks its criteria from its delegate.  A criterion with
  brothers is shown in a pop-up of them all, one with none as a label, and
  a view as it is.  Choosing another criterion walks the row on from it,
  keeping the titles that follow where it can.  Each row has a plus, which
  adds a row beneath a compound row or after a simple one, and a minus.
  Choosing, adding and removing send the action.  The editor is flipped
  and as tall as its rows.
- A predicate editor has no delegate: it is its own, over a tree of its
  templates' pop-ups, where items with the same title at the same place
  are one criterion.  Its display values are titles, and the views of the
  row's own copy of its template.  Choosing a criterion of another template
  switches the row to it.  Typing in a row sends the action.  Setting a
  predicate leaves out the parts no template can show, at any depth, a
  compound that loses them all keeping its row; a predicate no template
  can show at all, or a NOT whose part cannot be shown, raises
  NSInvalidArgumentException.  New templates keep the rows they can show.
- A compound template gives a compound of the rows, of one or of none
  too (an AND of none holds, an OR of none does not), and a compound row
  with no rows beneath it is such a predicate; NOT of several is NOT of
  their OR.
- Templates for key paths: key paths of the same type, one after
  another, share a template, with OS X's operators for that type, through
  relationships; a binary attribute or a key with no attribute raises.  The entity is only sent messages, since
  GNUstep has no CoreData of its own.
- A keyed archive keeps a rule editor's nesting mode, row height,
  editability and whether all rows can go (NSRuleEditor... keys), and a
  template as its views, whose items stand for what they offer
  (NSPredicateTemplate... keys, NSRepObject for a menu item's object).
  A xib keeps the same as nestingMode, rowHeight, canRemoveAllRows,
  rowTemplates, rowType, rightExpressionObject and the menus of
  popUpMenus, with <expression> elements, which the xib parser makes
  objects of NSExpression, a cluster that cannot read a xib.

The tests, checked against OS X: NSPredicateEditor/criteria.m (56),
NSPredicateEditorRowTemplate/keyPaths.m (32), NSRuleEditor/rows.m (26),
NSRuleEditor/targetAction.m (6) and GSXib5KeyedUnarchiver/predicateEditor.m
with PredicateEditor.xib, a fetch request's editor from a Core Data model
editor (24).  Without the change criteria.m and rows.m abort, and 44
of the other three's 62 checks fail.  The existing NSRuleEditor, NSPredicateEditor and
NSPredicateEditorRowTemplate tests pass as before, and so does the rest of
the gui suite (5056 passed).

